  "guidebook": {
    "intro": "Real situations take **bwe** (when, if). Imagined ones take **singa** (if) and the conditional **-andi-** (would).",
    "phrases": [
      { "target": "Bwe nzuukuka, nnywa caayi.", "base": "When I wake up, I drink tea." },
      { "target": "Enkuba bw’etonnya, sigenda.", "base": "When it rains, I don’t go." },
      { "target": "Singa nnali nnina ssente, nnandiguze mmotoka.", "base": "If I had money, I would buy a car." },
      { "target": "Nnandyagadde caayi, nsaba.", "base": "I would like tea, please." },
      { "target": "Ka tulabe enkya.", "base": "Let’s see tomorrow." },
      { "target": "Nja kujja, Katonda bw’anaayagala.", "base": "I will come, God willing." }
    ],
    "tips": [
      {
        "id": "bwe",
        "title": "When: bwe",
        "body": [
          "Put **bwe** before the verb for “when” or “whenever”: **Bwe nzuukuka** (when I wake up), **Bwe ntuuka** (when I arrive). With a noun, it goes after the noun and joins the verb: **Enkuba bw’etonnya** (when the rain falls)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "singa",
        "title": "If: singa and -andi-",
        "body": [
          "For things that aren’t true, start with **singa** and put the result in the conditional with **-andi-**: **nnandiguze** (I would buy), **nnandigenze** (I would go), **wandiguze** (you would buy).",
          "**Nnandyagadde** (I would like) is the politest way to ask for something: **Nnandyagadde caayi**."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "culture": [
      {
        "title": "God willing",
        "body": [
          "You met **Katonda bw’anaayagala** in Unit 20. It literally means “if God wants”, built with **bwe**, and follows almost every promise about the future."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
